well february is black history month and  many in our community are looking to  support black owned businesses  here in las vegas new businesses are  popping up despite the challenges of the  pandemic  kate houston is live from charleston and  casino center where munchie's smokehouse  barbecue  is serving up some unique dishes in a  pretty unusual time  kate  yes that's right we're here at munchies  smoked house where they're taking  classic barbecue  and they're really just adding a fun  twist to it with dishes like tri-tip mac  and cheese  barbecue ramen and their fan favorite  rib tips  as a recently opened black-owned  business they're hoping to cultivate a  community here of all different styles  of food  i think our concepts are really fun and  they're new this is a tri-tip  for the past four months chaute ducharme  and brandon jackson have been selling a  wide variety of barbecue dishes from  their new food truck  munchies smokehouse barbecue we just  wanted to do a very different fusion  style barbecue  we make nachos out of 20 different chips  it's all a munchie style  what started as a dream soon became a  necessity when chaute lost her job  right before the pandemic was just  scared for my family  really honestly scared of like where we  were going to be if we were going to be  homeless  so i had to start thinking about what we  were going to do  to make some money after a few setbacks  the food truck made its debut this past  october  since then the lines haven't stopped  it's a great feeling it went from  not knowing if it was going to be a good  decision to  with super bowl coming up just turning  away too many people  as a black-owned business jackson says  many customers have stopped by to try  their food for that very reason  and congratulate them on their success  and our smoker can hold 600 pounds of  meat at one time  now the duo is looking to support other  black-owned businesses through a  pan-african food truck night  really just try to create something here  in las vegas i think that  there definitely needs to be something  that incorporates everybody not just  black but we're talking about like the  afro  latinos and our caribbean folks and all  of that  although the food truck has only been  open for a short time they're looking at  the possibility  of a brick and mortar location and  potentially franchising  to other parts of the united states  reporting from charleston and south  casino boulevard tonight kate houston  8 news now
 

